  "narrative": "The second binary-predictor discovery wave (H13-H24) largely repeats the first wave's pattern: small unadjusted Welch contrasts, intervals crossing zero, and point estimates below the approximate 10%-of-outcome-magnitude screen for most outcomes. I interpret the returned estimates as already signed to the claim direction, so positive values support stronger dependency in the exposed group and negative values oppose the claim. For stable negative dependency means, I used an absolute screen of about 10% of the mean magnitude: roughly 0.025 for many standard outcomes, about 0.042 for outcome_001, and about 0.030 for outcome_011. For outcome_014, where the mean is near zero and variance is high, percentage thresholds are unstable, so I used absolute uncertainty cautiously rather than treating any crossing of a small percentage threshold as decisive. H13, H14, H16, H18, H20, H22, and H23 are rejected and closed because the signed point estimates are small, often opposite to the claimed direction, and the upper signed bounds fall below the approximate clinical threshold for the relevant outcome; these intervals therefore do not leave plausible room for a clinically meaningful association in the hypothesized direction. H17, H21, and H24 are left unresolved and deferred: their point estimates are below the practical threshold and intervals include zero, but upper signed bounds remain above the rough 10% screen, so precise rejection is not justified. H15 is unresolved and deferred because outcome_014 is near zero and highly variable; the interval permits both no effect and effects large on a relative scale, although the absolute estimate is modest and the observed direction opposes the hypothesis. H19 is the strongest borderline candidate: outcome_015 feature_027 has a positive signed estimate of 0.0177, close to the approximate 0.025 threshold, with an interval nearly excluding zero on the lower end and extending well above the clinical screen on the upper end. Because the point estimate is not itself at threshold and many binary comparisons have been screened without adjustment, H19 is not accepted on discovery evidence alone; I request independent validation for H19 to distinguish a possible dataset-specific signal from a multiplicity-driven false lead. All conclusions remain associations in the supplied dataset, not causal effects or generalizable biological claims.",
